{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-10947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=1+2(x-\\frac{1}{2})+{2}^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{3}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-10947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }2|x-\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =2|x-\\frac{1}{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-\\frac{1}{2}|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-10947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f396a2aedcd2dde36e1f615af8184df311f096c0ad4b8ed4cac1acbc5af211f"} {"question" "q-10947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-10947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "R=1"] ["math" "R=2"] ["math" "R=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "R=\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "R=\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-10947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "R=\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}=(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{(x-1)^{6}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(x-1)^{9}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f396a2aedcd2dde36e1f615af8184df311f096c0ad4b8ed4cac1acbc5af211f"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "R=0"] ["math" "R=1"] ["math" "R=2"] ["math" "R=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "R=\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-13835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "R=\\infty "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-2.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-3$\n- The series diverges at $x=1$\n- The series converges at $x=8$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The center of the expansion is $x=3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-2,8).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/assets/38763aa0d87065a01ccdf0d94bca2a4e1d6ef227aeab4aaacb9703246eee6abf.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-3\\notin [-2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $1\\in [-2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [-2,8),$  we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements must be true.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f396a2aedcd2dde36e1f615af8184df311f096c0ad4b8ed4cac1acbc5af211f"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-50877" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None of the listed"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f396a2aedcd2dde36e1f615af8184df311f096c0ad4b8ed4cac1acbc5af211f"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$R=1$ and $I=(-6,-4)$"] ["text" "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4)$"] ["text" "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$"] ["text" "$R=5$ and $I=(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$R=5$ and $I=[-1,1]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+7)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-11$\n- The series converges at $x=-8$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The center of the expansion is $x=-7,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-11,-3].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-11,-3].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/assets/4764256e5f00e7a11d688bd99d5fa1c22db819e7a188113f0ef4cd987a9b7595.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-11\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-8\\in (-11,-3],$ the series is convergent at $x=-8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6f396a2aedcd2dde36e1f615af8184df311f096c0ad4b8ed4cac1acbc5af211f"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"} {"question" "q-103170" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/state.json" "file_sha256" "03256d18b6f3be66c168869a681a8d30041902248fbcff0911e062654921b4eb"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1840 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
